{
  "final_quality": "tie",
  "reason": "Both final plans support the same consequential outcome: independent local validation and preview can proceed, publication remains disabled, and service integration requires owner-supplied approved sandbox and contract evidence. Both retain separate post-implementation live acceptance for atomicity, unrelated-SKU preservation, interruption, retry and recovery. Neither treats offline failure, local probes or structural checks as service readiness. Their different decomposition, documentation volume and experiments do not demonstrate a final-quality advantage.",
  "decisive_evidence": [
    {
      "side": "A",
      "quote": "The offline transport sends no request and provides no evidence of remote atomicity",
      "locator": "A.plans[0].final.assumptions[A2]",
      "consequence": "Correctly limits source and offline observations without inferring either service atomicity or its absence."
    },
    {
      "side": "A",
      "quote": "CLI --preview FILE validates completely and emits deterministic unpublished JSON; --publish FILE refuses with PR-SERVICE-READY nonzero and never calls transport.",
      "locator": "A.plans[0].final.work_items[W3].context",
      "consequence": "Defines useful independent local delivery while preserving the publication gate."
    },
    {
      "side": "A",
      "quote": "Service-native probe result does not close TC_8–TC_11 candidate acceptance.",
      "locator": "A.plans[0].final.work_items[W4].context",
      "consequence": "Separates prerequisite contract evidence from still-due acceptance of the implemented importer."
    },
    {
      "side": "B",
      "quote": "READY: local Python/unittest available and baseline unchanged; no dependency on sandbox.",
      "locator": "B.plans[0].final.work_items[W1].context",
      "consequence": "Keeps local validation eligible despite the unavailable external boundary."
    },
    {
      "side": "B",
      "quote": "Missing owner evidence blocks this item before service-dependent design, adapter authoring, live test registration or calls; current authority cannot satisfy it.",
      "locator": "B.plans[0].final.work_items[W3].context",
      "consequence": "Makes INVENTORY_SERVICE_READY an actual integration prerequisite without inventing present authority."
    },
    {
      "side": "B",
      "quote": "Owner-native contract probes precede integration, candidate live acceptance follows implementation, preventing a readiness cycle.",
      "locator": "B.plans[0].final.summary",
      "consequence": "Establishes the same sound readiness-versus-acceptance ordering as A."
    },
    {
      "side": "B",
      "quote": "candidate importer passes TC_06 success, TC_07 service-side partial failure, TC_08 interrupted publication and TC_09 retry/recovery at real sandbox",
      "locator": "B.plans[0].final.work_items[W3].context, DONE",
      "consequence": "Retains all required live scenarios as future completion conditions, not observed successes."
    }
  ],
  "initial_plan_and_rework": "Both initial plans already contain the consequential local/service separation and native-readiness-before-candidate-acceptance ordering. A's earlier test-strategy review made meaningful repairs to circular readiness wording, a contradictory summary and the preview oracle; those repairs predate its initial plan. During plan review, A clarified A3 ownership, added explicit A8 tracking, documented fixture facilities and named acknowledged-but-unprocessed interruption. Initial A3/W4/W5 already required schema reconciliation and standard-library compatibility, and W4 already addressed accepted-but-unprocessed recovery, so these changes mainly improved visibility rather than introduced missing gates. The subsequent FC_1 registry correction was bookkeeping. B's plan review added only an investigation evidence reference, with no semantic change; its producer had already clarified readiness ordering. B therefore shows less review rework, separately from the tied final quality. Five work items versus three is not itself a quality distinction.",
  "earlier_evidence_value": "The supplied request and inspected source already establish that pre-send failure cannot reveal the service contract. Reusing that evidence is legitimate. Recorded baseline passes and unchanged source/test checks support preserving the existing offline behavior, not feature acceptance. A's unittest-output probe answers a limited traceability question. B's parser probe usefully identifies permissive JSON defaults and boolean/integer behavior, informing explicit validation checks; it establishes neither an implemented validator nor remote compatibility. A can legitimately specify equivalent strict checks without repeating that experiment. Both correctly reuse available evidence and select zero additional planning experiments for questions that require unavailable service evidence.",
  "harms_or_lost_requirements": [
    "No consequential lost requirement, unauthorized service operation or false readiness claim is demonstrated. Unimplemented feature checks and unavailable live evidence remain explicitly outstanding, as appropriate for this planning slice.",
    "Both plans use one-application or one-commit language for retry acceptance. If interpreted as prohibiting harmless repeated physical execution rather than duplicate logical effects, this could overconstrain the requested idempotence. That is a shared interpretation risk, not an established B-only regression.",
    "Retained owned scratch evidence has no demonstrated effect on experiment validity, authority, sensitive data or running resources. Missing deletion language alone establishes no material harm. Extra invocations, rereads and registry checks are overhead unless a consequential effect is shown."
  ],
  "individual_judgment_corrections": [
    "B's judgment overstates the evidence when it translates 'exactly one logical application' into an established requirement for 'single execution.' The wording warrants scrutiny, especially its reference to concealed duplicate execution, but the plan does not explicitly prohibit repeated requests or internal attempts.",
    "The idempotence concern cannot fairly be treated as unique to B: A also requires 'exactly one effective commit after recovery' in W5 and 'exactly one commit' in its live success procedure. A's empty unsupported-commitments list does not establish a comparative advantage on this point.",
    "The overall N1–N5 conclusions are supported. A's individual judgment correctly recognizes that its plan-review assumption changes largely clarify prerequisites already present; the review notes' 'material' classifications do not independently demonstrate consequential repairs."
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "limits": [
    "The full command transcript is omitted. Exact command-level overhead claims, particularly B's alleged redundant framework rereads and the detailed sequencing of A's standalone stub invocation, cannot be independently re-audited here. They remain reported cost observations rather than demonstrated regressions.",
    "Neither plan implements or empirically accepts the new feature. The service schema, atomicity, transport compatibility, authorization and recovery facilities remain unknown; future live evidence may require replanning.",
    "This comparison supports a tie in conditional planning quality, not a release decision or a general causal improvement claim."
  ]
}
